.TH LITECAST 1 "September 2026" "litecast 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
litecast \- stream media to a Chromecast from the local machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B litecast
.RB [ \-b " "\fISIZE\fP | "\-\-cache" " "\fISIZE\fP ]
.RB [ \-s | \-\-cache\-to\-fs ]
.RB [ \-d " "\fINAME\fP | "\-\-device" " "\fINAME\fP ]
.RB [ \-n | \-\-no\-arbitrary\-range ]
.RB ( \-f | \-\-file " | " \-c | \-\-command )
.RB [ \-\- ]
.IR FILES... " | " command-and-args..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B litecast
discovers a Chromecast on the local network, connects to its Cast V2
endpoint, and serves a local media source purely as a source: the device
pulls the stream, litecast only feeds it from an in-memory buffer and an
optional spool file.
.PP
No media validation is performed: container, codec, and SPS/PPS are never
inspected, and the bytes are served verbatim.
.PP
In file mode operands are streamed one after another, `cat`\fB-\fPstyle, as a
single concatenated stream. In command mode the operand list is executed
directly with `fork`/`execvp` (never through a shell) and its standard
output is cast.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-b " SIZE ", " "\-\-cache " SIZE
Buffer size for the stream. Accepts a `K`/`M`/`G` suffix (case-insensitive)
or a trailing `%`. Default `64M`; minimum one page; a percentage is capped
at `100%` (values above are an error). In file mode a plain size is a
fixed-capacity in-memory buffer; a `%` targets a fraction of the file's
size (only trustworthy for regular files). In command mode the buffer is
always dynamic, starting at one page and growing toward the configured
size; `100%` grows with every byte, retaining the whole stream in memory.
.TP
.BI "\-s " ", " "\-\-cache\-to\-fs"
Only meaningful in command mode (ignored in file mode). Spools 100% of the
command output to a temp file that is created, opened, and immediately
unlinked (a dangling file descriptor). The spool keeps a full copy of the
command output on disk, independent of the buffer policy; the cast feed
itself is live and non-seekable: `Content-Length` appears only once the
command completes, and a `206` `Range` answer only for a closed range (the
request pins the end, or `total` is known); see `\-n` for open-ended
`Range` requests.
.TP
.BI "\-d " NAME ", " "\-\-device " NAME
Select the cast target by friendly name. Matching is case-insensitive,
with `*` as the only wildcard; a bare name falls back to substring
matching (`*NAME*`). If no device matches within the discovery window the
first discovered device is used. Without this option the first discovered
device is used.
.TP
.B "\-n, \-\-no\-arbitrary\-range"
Never choose the end of an open-ended `Range: bytes=N-` request. By default
a stream of unknown length answers such a request with a legal `206` whose
`Content-Range` end the server picks: the produced frontier once the bytes
cover `N`, or `N+1` (one byte, held back until the producer reaches it)
when a running producer is behind. This option forbids that and answers
every open-ended `Range` on an unknown-length stream as a plain `200` from
byte 0, restarting the device at the beginning instead of continuing where
it stopped. Use it with a device that treats a short `206` as the end of
the stream. No effect in file mode, where the total length is known up
front; before the first byte is produced, or for a start already dropped
from the buffer, `200` from byte 0 is used either way.
.TP
.B "\-f, \-\-file"
Cast files (the default mode). Operands are treated as paths.
.TP
.B "\-c, \-\-command"
Cast the standard output of a command. Operands are treated as the command
and its arguments. Incompatible with `--file`; the last mode option wins.
.TP
.B "\-\-"
End of option parsing. Every following token is an operand, even if it
starts with `\-`.
.TP
.B "\-h, \-\-help"
Print usage to standard output and exit 0.
.TP
.B "\-V, \-\-version"
Print the version to standard output and exit 0.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean exit: the stream finished, or `--help`/`--version` was requested.
.TP
.B 1
Any failure: bad arguments, no device discovered, connection failure, or
stream error.
